strategy.content:
India's National Logistics Policy from 2022 envisions a technologically enabled, integrated, cost efficient, resilient, sustainable and trusted logistics ecosystem for accelerated and inclusive growth in India. Key objectives of the policy include integration, optimisation, standardisation, modernisation, formalisation, and democratisation. Targets for achieving the vision of the National Logistics Policy are to:
- Reduce cost of logistics in India to be comparable to global benchmarks by 2030;
- Improve the Logistics Performance Index ranking to be among top 25 countries by 2030;
- Create data driven decision support mechanism for an efficient logistics ecosystem.
Some of the strategies to achieve the targets include adopting a more efficient, economical, and environmentally sustainable modal mix, and multimodal interconnected infrastructure.

Viet Nam's logistics service development strategy for the period 2025-2035, with a vision to 2050 (Decision No. 2229/QĐ-TTg) aims to develop Viet Nam's logistics services to be sustainable, efficient, high-quality, and high value-added, with strong competitiveness, leveraging Viet Nam's advantages in global supply and value chains. Inaugurated in 2025, the policy supersedes the 2022 Decision 876/QĐ-TTg on Green Transportation with a freight transport focused policy.
Some of the policy’s specific objectives include:
- Developing logistics services efficiently in the direction of reducing emissions, contributing to bringing the nation's net emissions to zero;
- 12-15% average annual growth rate of the logistics service industry;
- 12%-15% decrease in logistics costs relative to the gross domestic product;
- By 2035, reach 5-7% value added of logistics services in the gross domestic product;
- By 2050, 70% of workers in the logistics service industry have received technical and professional training, with 30% holding a university degree or higher;
- Establish at least five modern, internationally-standard logistics service centers, serving as hubs for connecting and developing supply chains in the region and internationally.
